export default JSON.parse("{\"2.10.1\":{\"unit\":\"2.10\",\"courseId\":\"2.10.1\",\"bridge\":\"In 2.9.8 a weekend gap skipped your stop. Scheduled releases can do the same, at a minute you can now convert (2.9.1). First, why price jumps at all: on the surprise against what was expected, sized against normal noise, as 0.2 taught.\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"A synthetic jobs report beats consensus by 60,000 jobs. Another beats it by 10,000. Both are 'better than expected'. Which one usually moves price more?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"first\",\"label\":\"The first, by 60,000\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Measured against the usual size of surprises, the first is six times bigger.\"},{\"id\":\"second\",\"label\":\"The second, by 10,000\",\"feedback\":\"A smaller beat is, if anything, more likely to be noise. The first is six times bigger.\"},{\"id\":\"same\",\"label\":\"The same: both beat\",\"feedback\":\"Both beat, but by very different amounts. Price reacts to how big the surprise is.\"},{\"id\":\"cant\",\"label\":\"Can't tell\",\"feedback\":\"You can, roughly: compare each beat with the usual size of surprises. The first is six times bigger.\"}],\"answer\":\"first\"},\"compare\":\"You chose {yours}. If surprises in this report usually vary by about 60,000, the first is z = 1.0 and the second z = 0.17: inside the noise.\",\"concept\":\"news-surprise\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"warning\",\"text\":\"Risk first: every scheduled release is a volatility event before it is information. Spreads widen and price can jump past your stop, whatever the number says (2.10.3). Understanding the surprise doesn't make the jump any safer.\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Priced in\",\"text\":\"Before a release, economists publish forecasts; their middle is the [[consensus]]. Traders position for it in advance, so the expected number is largely [[priced-in]]. What's new on the day is the [[surprise]]: actual minus consensus. Good news that everyone expected moves price little. Better-than-expected news can.\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Sizing a surprise\",\"lines\":[\"Consensus: 150,000 jobs. Actual: 210,000.\",\"Surprise = actual − consensus = +60,000.\",\"In this report, surprises usually vary by about 60,000: that's σ, their standard deviation.\",\"Standardised surprise z = surprise ÷ σ = 60,000 ÷ 60,000 = +1.0.\",\"Another month: actual 160,000. z = 10,000 ÷ 60,000 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":0.17,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"feedback\":[{\"value\":10000,\"text\":\"That's the surprise. Divide it by σ, 60,000, to compare it with the usual size.\"},{\"value\":6,\"text\":\"That's 60,000 ÷ 10,000. It's the surprise that's divided by σ.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: z = 0.17, a sixth of a usual surprise.\"},\"after\":\"z puts every surprise on one scale: how many usual surprises it is. A z of 1.0 is a real miss for the forecasters. A z of 0.17 is the kind of gap that happens most months.\",\"concept\":\"news-surprise\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"The jump scales with the surprise\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"chart\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":100,\"h\":100.06,\"l\":99.96,\"c\":100.04},{\"o\":100.04,\"h\":100.1,\"l\":100,\"c\":100.06},{\"o\":100.06,\"h\":100.12,\"l\":100.02,\"c\":100.1},{\"o\":100.1,\"h\":100.14,\"l\":100.04,\"c\":100.08},{\"o\":100.08,\"h\":100.16,\"l\":100.06,\"c\":100.14},{\"o\":100.14,\"h\":100.2,\"l\":100.1,\"c\":100.18},{\"o\":100.18,\"h\":100.22,\"l\":100.12,\"c\":100.16},{\"o\":100.16,\"h\":100.26,\"l\":100.14,\"c\":100.24},{\"o\":100.24,\"h\":100.3,\"l\":100.2,\"c\":100.28},{\"o\":100.28,\"h\":101.3,\"l\":100.24,\"c\":101.1},{\"o\":101.1,\"h\":101.26,\"l\":100.96,\"c\":101.04},{\"o\":101.04,\"h\":101.2,\"l\":100.98,\"c\":101.16},{\"o\":101.16,\"h\":101.24,\"l\":101.06,\"c\":101.12},{\"o\":101.12,\"h\":101.22,\"l\":101.04,\"c\":101.18}]},\"steps\":[{\"text\":\"Before the release, price drifts as traders position for the expected number.\"},{\"text\":\"At T0 the actual beats consensus by one usual surprise, z = +1.0. Price jumps.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":9,\"at\":\"high\",\"label\":\"T0 · z +1.0\",\"role\":\"accent\"}]},{\"text\":\"Had it been z = 0.17, inside the noise band, the move would likely drown in ordinary wiggles.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"path\",\"role\":\"neutral\",\"dashed\":true,\"label\":\"IF z = 0.17\",\"points\":[{\"index\":9,\"price\":100.28},{\"index\":10,\"price\":100.36},{\"index\":11,\"price\":100.24},{\"index\":12,\"price\":100.32},{\"index\":13,\"price\":100.28}]}]}]},\"text\":\"An illustration. At major releases the first move tends to follow the sign of the surprise, but its size is noisy. In this course, |z| under 0.5 counts as noise.\",\"concept\":\"news-surprise\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Hot or cold?\",\"prompt\":\"'Hot' means a stronger economy or higher inflation than expected. Sort each reading. Careful: for some series, the lower number is the hot one.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"CPI 0.4% vs 0.3% expected\",\"right\":\"Hot\",\"wrong\":\"Inflation above consensus is hot: it makes higher rates more likely.\"},{\"left\":\"Unemployment rate 3.9% vs 4.1% expected\",\"right\":\"Hot\",\"wrong\":\"Lower unemployment than expected means a tighter job market: hot, although the number is lower.\"},{\"left\":\"Jobless claims 250,000 vs 220,000 expected\",\"right\":\"Cold\",\"wrong\":\"More benefit claims than expected means more layoffs: cold, although the number is higher.\"},{\"left\":\"Payrolls 90,000 vs 150,000 expected\",\"right\":\"Cold\",\"wrong\":\"Fewer jobs added than expected: a colder labour market.\"}],\"columns\":[\"Reading\",\"Hot or cold\"],\"correct\":\"Right. Read which way each series counts before you read the sign of the surprise.\",\"concept\":\"news-surprise\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"Good news is bad news\",\"prompt\":\"LABX trades like a stock index. Payrolls beat consensus by a wide margin. The market's main fear is inflation: strong jobs mean higher rates for longer. What does LABX usually do?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"up\",\"label\":\"Rises: strong jobs are good news\",\"feedback\":\"That's the textbook reading. With inflation the main fear, strong jobs mean higher rates, and that weighs on stocks.\"},{\"id\":\"down\",\"label\":\"Falls: strong jobs mean higher rates\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Seen through the market's main fear, good news for jobs is bad news for rates.\"},{\"id\":\"flat\",\"label\":\"Doesn't move: jobs don't affect stocks\",\"feedback\":\"A wide beat on a top release usually moves risk assets. The question is which way.\"}],\"answer\":\"down\"},\"reveal\":\"Usually down. When inflation is the main fear, strong jobs mean higher rates for longer, which weighs on stock prices. In a recession scare, the same beat calms nerves and stocks tend to rise. Same surprise, different fear, opposite move.\",\"concept\":\"news-surprise\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"key\",\"text\":\"The calendar tells you when. The surprise tells you how much. What the market fears decides which way.\"}],\"task\":{\"widget\":\"bias-spotter\",\"data\":{\"options\":[{\"id\":\"none\",\"label\":\"No clear move (|z| under 0.5)\"},{\"id\":\"up-med\",\"label\":\"LABX up, medium (|z| 0.5–1.5)\"},{\"id\":\"up-big\",\"label\":\"LABX up, large (|z| over 1.5)\"},{\"id\":\"down-med\",\"label\":\"LABX down, medium (|z| 0.5–1.5)\"},{\"id\":\"down-big\",\"label\":\"LABX down, large (|z| over 1.5)\"}],\"cards\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"text\":\"**Payrolls** · consensus 150,000 · actual 230,000 · usual surprise σ 60,000 · market focus: growth, after weak data\",\"answer\":\"up-med\",\"correct\":\"Right. z = 80,000 ÷ 60,000 = +1.33: medium. With growth the worry, strong jobs are good news for LABX.\",\"rationales\":{\"none\":\"No clear move fits |z| under 0.5. Here z = 80,000 ÷ 60,000 = +1.33, and with growth the worry, strong jobs are good news for LABX.\",\"up-big\":\"Up, large fits |z| over 1.5, on news LABX likes. Here z = 80,000 ÷ 60,000 = +1.33, and with growth the worry, strong jobs are good news for LABX.\",\"down-med\":\"Down, medium fits |z| from 0.5 to 1.5, on news LABX fears. Here z = 80,000 ÷ 60,000 = +1.33, and with growth the worry, strong jobs are good news for LABX.\",\"down-big\":\"Down, large fits |z| over 1.5, on news LABX fears. Here z = 80,000 ÷ 60,000 = +1.33, and with growth the worry, strong jobs are good news for LABX.\"}},{\"id\":\"b\",\"text\":\"**Payrolls** · consensus 180,000 · actual 200,000 · σ 60,000 · market focus: growth\",\"answer\":\"none\",\"correct\":\"Right. z = 20,000 ÷ 60,000 = +0.33: inside the noise band. Any wiggle is unlikely to mean much.\",\"rationales\":{\"up-med\":\"Up, medium fits |z| from 0.5 to 1.5, on news LABX likes. Here z = 20,000 ÷ 60,000 = +0.33, inside the noise band.\",\"up-big\":\"Up, large fits |z| over 1.5, on news LABX likes. Here z = 20,000 ÷ 60,000 = +0.33, inside the noise band.\",\"down-med\":\"Down, medium fits |z| from 0.5 to 1.5, on news LABX fears. Here z = 20,000 ÷ 60,000 = +0.33, inside the noise band.\",\"down-big\":\"Down, large fits |z| over 1.5, on news LABX fears. Here z = 20,000 ÷ 60,000 = +0.33, inside the noise band.\"}},{\"id\":\"c\",\"text\":\"**Core CPI**, month on month · consensus 0.3% · actual 0.5% · σ 0.1 · market focus: inflation\",\"answer\":\"down-big\",\"correct\":\"Right. z = 0.2 ÷ 0.1 = +2.0: large. With inflation the fear, hot CPI means higher rates, and LABX falls.\",\"rationales\":{\"none\":\"No clear move fits |z| under 0.5. Here z = 0.2 ÷ 0.1 = +2.0, and with inflation the fear, hot CPI is bad news for LABX.\",\"up-med\":\"Up, medium fits |z| from 0.5 to 1.5, on news LABX likes. Here z = 0.2 ÷ 0.1 = +2.0, and with inflation the fear, hot CPI is bad news for LABX.\",\"up-big\":\"Up, large fits |z| over 1.5, on news LABX likes. Here z = 0.2 ÷ 0.1 = +2.0, and with inflation the fear, hot CPI is bad news for LABX.\",\"down-med\":\"Down, medium fits |z| from 0.5 to 1.5, on news LABX fears. Here z = 0.2 ÷ 0.1 = +2.0, and with inflation the fear, hot CPI is bad news for LABX.\"}},{\"id\":\"d\",\"text\":\"**Core CPI**, month on month · consensus 0.3% · actual 0.1% · σ 0.1 · market focus: inflation\",\"answer\":\"up-big\",\"correct\":\"Right. z = −0.2 ÷ 0.1 = −2.0: large and cool. With inflation the fear, cooler prices mean lower rates, and LABX rises.\",\"rationales\":{\"none\":\"No clear move fits |z| under 0.5. Here z = −0.2 ÷ 0.1 = −2.0, and with inflation the fear, cool CPI is good news for LABX.\",\"up-med\":\"Up, medium fits |z| from 0.5 to 1.5, on news LABX likes. Here z = −0.2 ÷ 0.1 = −2.0, and with inflation the fear, cool CPI is good news for LABX.\",\"down-med\":\"Down, medium fits |z| from 0.5 to 1.5, on news LABX fears. Here z = −0.2 ÷ 0.1 = −2.0, and with inflation the fear, cool CPI is good news for LABX.\",\"down-big\":\"Down, large fits |z| over 1.5, on news LABX fears. Here z = −0.2 ÷ 0.1 = −2.0, and with inflation the fear, cool CPI is good news for LABX.\"}},{\"id\":\"e\",\"text\":\"**Payrolls** · consensus 150,000 · actual 220,000 · σ 60,000 · market focus: inflation\",\"answer\":\"down-med\",\"correct\":\"Right. z = 70,000 ÷ 60,000 = +1.17: medium. With inflation the fear, strong jobs mean higher rates: good news is bad news.\",\"rationales\":{\"none\":\"No clear move fits |z| under 0.5. Here z = 70,000 ÷ 60,000 = +1.17, and with inflation the fear, strong jobs are bad news for LABX.\",\"up-med\":\"Up, medium fits |z| from 0.5 to 1.5, on news LABX likes. Here z = 70,000 ÷ 60,000 = +1.17, and with inflation the fear, strong jobs are bad news for LABX.\",\"up-big\":\"Up, large fits |z| over 1.5, on news LABX likes. Here z = 70,000 ÷ 60,000 = +1.17, and with inflation the fear, strong jobs are bad news for LABX.\",\"down-big\":\"Down, large fits |z| over 1.5, on news LABX fears. Here z = 70,000 ÷ 60,000 = +1.17, and with inflation the fear, strong jobs are bad news for LABX.\"}}]},\"goal\":\"Five synthetic releases. For each, compute z, read the market's focus, and pick LABX's likely first move.\",\"success\":{\"field\":\"solved\",\"eq\":5},\"correct\":\"Five for five. Size comes from z, direction from what the market fears. These are tendencies, not promises: a first move can still fail.\",\"hints\":[\"z = (actual − consensus) ÷ σ. Under 0.5 is noise, 0.5 to 1.5 medium, over 1.5 large.\",\"If inflation is the fear, hot data is bad for LABX. If growth is the fear, strong data is good.\"],\"showMe\":{\"solved\":5},\"concept\":\"news-surprise\"},\"check\":[\"q-2.10.1-1\",\"q-2.10.1-2\",\"q-2.10.1-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Prices move on the surprise, actual minus consensus, because the consensus is already in the price.\",\"Standardise it: z = surprise ÷ the usual surprise size. 60,000 against a σ of 60,000 is z = 1.0.\",\"Surprises under half the usual size are mostly noise. Don't read a trend into them.\"],\"next\":\"Next, 2.10.2: the economic calendar, where the time, the consensus and the actual sit on one row.\"}},\"2.10.2\":{\"unit\":\"2.10\",\"courseId\":\"2.10.2\",\"bridge\":\"In 2.10.1 you measured a surprise as actual minus consensus. The economic calendar is where both numbers appear, with the release time and an impact rating. Here you read a row, convert its time with the DST trap from 2.9.1, and triage a week.\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"A synthetic jobs release is at 08:30 New York time. Europe's clocks went back last Sunday; the US clocks change next Sunday. What time is it in Bucharest?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"t1330\",\"label\":\"13:30\",\"feedback\":\"That's a 5-hour gap. New York is usually 7 hours behind Bucharest, and 6 this week.\"},{\"id\":\"t1430\",\"label\":\"14:30\",\"feedback\":\"Right. This week the gap is 6 hours, not the usual 7.\"},{\"id\":\"t1530\",\"label\":\"15:30\",\"feedback\":\"That's the usual time, with a 7-hour gap. This week Europe has changed its clocks and the US hasn't: 6 hours.\"},{\"id\":\"t1630\",\"label\":\"16:30\",\"feedback\":\"That's an 8-hour gap. New York is usually 7 hours behind Bucharest, and 6 this week.\"}],\"answer\":\"t1430\"},\"compare\":\"You chose {yours}. Usually 08:30 in New York is 15:30 in Bucharest. In the weeks when only one side has changed its clocks, it's 14:30.\",\"concept\":\"economic-calendar\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"warning\",\"text\":\"Risk first: the calendar reliably tells you **when** volatility, spreads and gap risk will rise. It doesn't tell you which way price goes, and unscheduled events never appear on it (2.10.9).\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"text\":\"An [[economic-calendar]] lists each upcoming release on one row: the time, an [[impact-tier]], the previous value, any [[revision]] to it, the [[consensus]] and, once published, the actual. Read a row as a volatility forecast: it says when the market will jump, and which number it expects.\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"The DST trap\",\"text\":\"US releases are set in New York time, usually 7 hours behind Bucharest: 08:30 there is 15:30 here. But the US and Europe change clocks on different Sundays. For two to three weeks in March, and one week at the turn of October and November, the gap is 6 hours: 08:30 becomes 14:30.\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"Read the row\",\"prompt\":\"Fri · 15:30 · Payrolls · Impact: red · Previous 140K · Revised 120K · Consensus 150K · Actual: not out yet. When the number lands, which field is it measured against?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"previous\",\"label\":\"Previous, 140K\",\"feedback\":\"Previous is last month's number, and the market has already traded it. What it expects now is the consensus.\"},{\"id\":\"revised\",\"label\":\"Revised, 120K\",\"feedback\":\"The revision corrects last month. It matters, but the surprise is measured against the consensus.\"},{\"id\":\"consensus\",\"label\":\"Consensus, 150K\",\"feedback\":\"Right.\"},{\"id\":\"impact\",\"label\":\"Impact, red\",\"feedback\":\"Impact rates how much the event usually moves markets. It isn't a number to compare with.\"}],\"answer\":\"consensus\"},\"reveal\":\"The consensus. The surprise is actual minus consensus, because the consensus is what's priced in. Previous and revised describe last month. The revision still counts: here last month was cut by 20K, and that changes what the new number means.\",\"concept\":\"economic-calendar\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"The fields\",\"prompt\":\"Match each field of a calendar row to what it holds.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"Previous\",\"right\":\"Last period's number, as first published\",\"wrong\":\"Previous is last period's figure as it was first released, before any correction.\"},{\"left\":\"Revised\",\"right\":\"Last period's number, corrected with fuller data\",\"wrong\":\"Revised replaces the previous figure once more data has come in.\"},{\"left\":\"Consensus\",\"right\":\"The middle of the forecasts: roughly what's priced in\",\"wrong\":\"Consensus is the forecasters' middle estimate, the number the actual is compared with.\"},{\"left\":\"Actual\",\"right\":\"The new number, filled in at release time\",\"wrong\":\"Actual stays empty until the release, then shows the published number.\"},{\"left\":\"Impact\",\"right\":\"The calendar's rating of how much it usually moves markets\",\"wrong\":\"Impact is the calendar's own rating, often red, orange or yellow.\"}],\"columns\":[\"Field\",\"What it holds\"],\"correct\":\"Right. Five fields, one question: what's new against what was expected.\",\"concept\":\"economic-calendar\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Revisions can flip the headline\",\"lines\":[\"Payrolls: consensus 150,000, actual 210,000. Headline surprise: +60,000.\",\"The two prior months are revised down by 120,000 in total.\",\"Net surprise, counting revisions: +60,000 − 120,000 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":-60000,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"jobs\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":60000,\"text\":\"That's the headline alone. Take off the 120,000 of downward revisions.\"},{\"value\":180000,\"text\":\"Downward revisions subtract from the news: +60,000 − 120,000.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: −60,000. Counting the revisions, the report is weaker than expected.\"},\"after\":\"The first spike usually follows the headline. As traders read the revisions, it can reverse. Read the whole row before you read the chart.\",\"concept\":\"economic-calendar\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Tiers are opinions\",\"text\":\"[[impact-tier|Impact tiers]] are editorial: each calendar rates events its own way, and what matters shifts with the regime. When inflation is the fear, CPI is the main event. Calendars also list speeches, holidays and bond auctions. And dates can move: a government shutdown, for example, can delay or cancel releases.\"},{\"kind\":\"order\",\"title\":\"Triage the week\",\"prompt\":\"Put your weekly calendar routine in order.\",\"items\":[\"List this week's red events for your instruments.\",\"Convert each time to your own time zone.\",\"Decide the policy for each: flat, reduce or ignore.\",\"Write the policies into your trading plan.\",\"Set an alarm 15 minutes before each red event.\"],\"explain\":\"Right. Decisions first, while you're calm, then the plan and the alarms. At T−15 minutes you act; you don't decide.\",\"concept\":\"economic-calendar\"}],\"task\":{\"widget\":\"bias-spotter\",\"data\":{\"options\":[{\"id\":\"flat\",\"label\":\"Flat by T−15\"},{\"id\":\"half\",\"label\":\"Half size\"},{\"id\":\"ignore\",\"label\":\"Ignore\"}],\"cards\":[{\"id\":\"mon\",\"text\":\"**Mon 16:45** · Manufacturing survey · orange\",\"answer\":\"half\",\"correct\":\"Half size. A survey like this can move LABX, but less than the red events.\",\"rationales\":{\"flat\":\"Flat by T−15 is for red events. This one is orange: half size.\",\"ignore\":\"Ignore is for yellow events. This one is orange: half size.\"}},{\"id\":\"tue\",\"text\":\"**Tue 17:00** · Consumer confidence · yellow\",\"answer\":\"ignore\",\"correct\":\"Ignore. A yellow event gets no action in this plan.\",\"rationales\":{\"flat\":\"Flat by T−15 is for red events. This one is yellow: no action.\",\"half\":\"Half size is for orange events. This one is yellow: no action.\"}},{\"id\":\"wed1\",\"text\":\"**Wed 15:30** · CPI · red\",\"answer\":\"flat\",\"correct\":\"Flat by 15:15. No new orders from then until after the release.\",\"rationales\":{\"half\":\"Half size is for orange events. CPI is red here: flat by 15:15.\",\"ignore\":\"Ignore is for yellow events. CPI is red here: flat by 15:15.\"}},{\"id\":\"wed2\",\"text\":\"**Wed 21:00** · Central bank rate decision · red\",\"answer\":\"flat\",\"correct\":\"Flat by 20:45. The decision, and the press conference after it, are red.\",\"rationales\":{\"half\":\"Half size is for orange events. The rate decision is red: flat by 20:45.\",\"ignore\":\"Ignore is for yellow events. The rate decision is red: flat by 20:45.\"}},{\"id\":\"thu1\",\"text\":\"**Thu 14:00** · Regional business survey · yellow\",\"answer\":\"ignore\",\"correct\":\"Ignore. Yellow events stay out of your way.\",\"rationales\":{\"flat\":\"Flat by T−15 is for red events. This one is yellow: no action.\",\"half\":\"Half size is for orange events. This one is yellow: no action.\"}},{\"id\":\"thu2\",\"text\":\"**Thu 15:30** · Weekly jobless claims · orange\",\"answer\":\"half\",\"correct\":\"Half size before 15:30.\",\"rationales\":{\"flat\":\"Flat by T−15 is for red events. This one is orange: half size.\",\"ignore\":\"Ignore is for yellow events. This one is orange: half size.\"}},{\"id\":\"thu3\",\"text\":\"**Thu 17:00** · Housing sales · yellow · plus, at the same minute, a speech by the central bank chair · orange\",\"answer\":\"half\",\"correct\":\"Half size. When two events share a minute, the higher tier sets the policy: orange.\",\"rationales\":{\"flat\":\"Flat by T−15 is for red events. Two events share 17:00, and the higher tier, orange, sets the policy.\",\"ignore\":\"The housing report alone is yellow, but an orange speech lands at the same minute. The higher tier sets the policy.\"}},{\"id\":\"fri1\",\"text\":\"**Fri 15:30** · Payrolls · red\",\"answer\":\"flat\",\"correct\":\"Flat by 15:15. Payrolls is one of the month's biggest events.\",\"rationales\":{\"half\":\"Half size is for orange events. Payrolls is red: flat by 15:15.\",\"ignore\":\"Ignore is for yellow events. Payrolls is red: flat by 15:15.\"}},{\"id\":\"fri2\",\"text\":\"**Fri 17:00** · Consumer sentiment · yellow\",\"answer\":\"ignore\",\"correct\":\"Ignore. Yellow, and the payrolls dust has had 90 minutes to settle.\",\"rationales\":{\"flat\":\"Flat by T−15 is for red events. This one is yellow: no action.\",\"half\":\"Half size is for orange events. This one is yellow: no action.\"}}]},\"goal\":\"Your plan: flat by T−15 before red events, half size before orange, ignore yellow. Triage nine LABX events, times in Bucharest.\",\"success\":{\"field\":\"solved\",\"eq\":9},\"correct\":\"All nine triaged, before the week starts. At T−15 the plan decides, so you don't have to.\",\"hints\":[\"Match each event's tier to the plan: red, flat; orange, half size; yellow, ignore.\",\"Two events at the same minute: the higher tier sets the policy.\"],\"showMe\":{\"solved\":9},\"concept\":\"economic-calendar\"},\"check\":[\"q-2.10.2-1\",\"q-2.10.2-2\",\"q-2.10.2-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Read the whole row: time, impact, previous, revised, consensus, actual. Revisions can flip the headline.\",\"Convert from New York time every week. In the clock-change gap weeks, 08:30 is 14:30, not 15:30.\",\"Triage the week before it starts: flat for red, half size for orange, ignore yellow.\"],\"next\":\"Next, 2.10.3: what spreads, slippage and stops do in the minute around a release.\"}},\"2.10.3\":{\"unit\":\"2.10\",\"courseId\":\"2.10.3\",\"bridge\":\"In 2.10.2 the calendar told you when volatility would rise. Here you see that minute up close: spreads widen, depth thins, and a triggered stop becomes a market order that fills after the jump, the slippage of 1.3.7 at news size.\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"Long 47 LABX at 100.00, stop 98.00, planned loss 98.66. At the release, the first bid after the jump is 96.80. What do you lose?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"slider\",\"min\":80,\"max\":200,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"vUSD\",\"start\":100,\"answer\":155,\"tolerance\":3,\"feedback\":[{\"from\":80,\"to\":104,\"text\":\"That's about the plan. But the stop didn't sell at 98.00: the first bid was 96.80.\"},{\"from\":105,\"to\":151,\"text\":\"More than the plan, but not enough: 47 units lost 3.20 each, plus commissions.\"},{\"from\":152,\"to\":158,\"text\":\"About right: 155.03, 1.57 times the plan.\"},{\"from\":159,\"to\":200,\"text\":\"Too much: 47 × 3.20 = 150.40, plus 4.63 of commissions.\"}]},\"compare\":\"You said {yours}. The stop sold at the first bid, 96.80: a loss of 155.03, 1.57 times the 98.66 you planned.\",\"concept\":\"news-liquidity\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Risk first: the minute around a release\",\"text\":\"In the last minute before a big release, dealers widen their quotes and pull resting orders: [[spread-widening]] to several times normal is routine on retail platforms. At the number, price can jump through levels where nobody is bidding. A triggered stop becomes a market order and fills at the first price there is.\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"Illustration: LABX's spread around a release\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"bars\",\"bars\":[{\"label\":\"T−60s\",\"value\":1,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"label\":\"T−30s\",\"value\":2,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"label\":\"T−10s\",\"value\":4,\"role\":\"down\"},{\"label\":\"T0\",\"value\":10,\"role\":\"down\"},{\"label\":\"T+30s\",\"value\":6,\"role\":\"down\"},{\"label\":\"T+60s\",\"value\":3,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"label\":\"T+90s\",\"value\":1.5,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"label\":\"T+120s\",\"value\":1,\"role\":\"neutral\"}],\"unit\":\"×\",\"steps\":[\"Spread in multiples of normal, 0.10. In the last minute, dealers widen quotes and pull orders.\",\"At T0 the spread hits 1.00, ten times normal. Depth is thinnest now.\",\"Within about two minutes it's back to 0.10. The worst fills come before that.\"]},\"text\":\"Liquidity usually returns within a minute or two. Until then, every fill pays a news-sized spread. Some brokers also raise margin requirements before big events.\",\"concept\":\"news-liquidity\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"The stop after the jump\",\"lines\":[\"Long 47 LABX at 100.00, stop 98.00. Planned loss with costs: 98.66, your 1R.\",\"At the release the bid jumps from above 98.00 straight to 96.80. Your stop sells there.\",\"Price loss: 47 × (100.00 − 96.80) = 150.40.\",\"Commissions: 2.35 in, 2.28 out.\",\"Actual loss: 150.40 + 2.35 + 2.28 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":155.03,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":150.4,\"text\":\"That's price alone. Add both commissions, 2.35 and 2.28.\"},{\"value\":98.66,\"text\":\"That's the plan, at 98.00. The stop filled at 96.80.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: 155.03.\"},\"after\":\"That's 56.37 over plan, about 0.57R of [[slippage]]: a −1.57R trade. Nothing traded between 98.00 and 96.80, so the stop took the first price there was.\",\"concept\":\"news-liquidity\"},{\"kind\":\"hotspot\",\"title\":\"A stop the mid never touched\",\"prompt\":\"Long, stop 98.00. The candles are mid prices; the dashed line is each minute's lowest bid. Tap the minute your stop was triggered.\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":98.9,\"h\":99,\"l\":98.84,\"c\":98.96},{\"o\":98.96,\"h\":99.04,\"l\":98.9,\"c\":98.94},{\"o\":98.94,\"h\":99,\"l\":98.86,\"c\":98.92},{\"o\":98.92,\"h\":98.98,\"l\":98.84,\"c\":98.88},{\"o\":98.88,\"h\":98.96,\"l\":98.82,\"c\":98.94},{\"o\":98.94,\"h\":99.02,\"l\":98.9,\"c\":98.98},{\"o\":98.98,\"h\":99.02,\"l\":98.88,\"c\":98.92},{\"o\":98.92,\"h\":98.96,\"l\":98.76,\"c\":98.8},{\"o\":98.8,\"h\":98.86,\"l\":98.4,\"c\":98.62},{\"o\":98.62,\"h\":98.84,\"l\":98.56,\"c\":98.78},{\"o\":98.78,\"h\":98.94,\"l\":98.72,\"c\":98.9},{\"o\":98.9,\"h\":99,\"l\":98.86,\"c\":98.96}],\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":98,\"role\":\"stop\",\"label\":\"STOP 98.00\"},{\"kind\":\"line\",\"values\":[98.79,98.85,98.81,98.79,98.77,98.85,98.78,98.56,97.9,98.26,98.57,98.81],\"role\":\"down\",\"dashed\":true,\"label\":\"LOWEST BID\"}]},\"zones\":[{\"id\":\"tm1\",\"label\":\"T−1\",\"index\":7,\"feedback\":\"Spread 0.40 here: the lowest bid was 98.56, above your stop.\"},{\"id\":\"t0\",\"label\":\"T0\",\"index\":8},{\"id\":\"tp1\",\"label\":\"T+1\",\"index\":9,\"feedback\":\"Spread 0.60, lowest bid 98.26: still above 98.00. The trigger came a minute earlier.\"},{\"id\":\"tp2\",\"label\":\"T+2\",\"index\":10,\"feedback\":\"Lowest bid 98.57. By then the stop had already sold.\"}],\"answer\":\"t0\",\"correct\":\"Right. At T0 the mid fell only to 98.40, but the spread hit 1.00, so the bid reached 97.90, under your stop. The mid never touched 98.00: a [[spread-triggered-stop]].\",\"concept\":\"news-liquidity\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"prompt\":\"So, at news time, does a stop cap your loss at its price?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"yes\",\"label\":\"Yes: that's what a stop is for\",\"feedback\":\"A stop picks the moment your order becomes a market sell. It doesn't pick the price.\"},{\"id\":\"no\",\"label\":\"No: it fills at the first bid it can get\",\"feedback\":\"Right.\"},{\"id\":\"limit\",\"label\":\"Only if it's a stop-limit\",\"feedback\":\"A stop-limit caps the price but may not fill at all in a jump, leaving you unprotected (1.3.3).\"}],\"answer\":\"no\"},\"reveal\":\"No. At news time the first price can sit far beyond your stop. Some brokers sell a [[guaranteed-stop]] for a premium; without one, size as if the gap will happen.\",\"concept\":\"news-liquidity\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"key\",\"text\":\"A stop is an instruction, not a price.\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Your turn: the news receipt\",\"lines\":[\"Plan: long 62 LABX at 100.50, stop 99.00. Loss at the stop with costs: 99.19, your 1R.\",\"Receipt: stop triggered at T0, filled at a bid of 97.40, with the spread at 0.80.\",\"Actual loss: 62 × 3.10 + 3.12 + 3.02 = 198.34.\",\"Slippage beyond the plan: 198.34 − 99.19 = 99.15.\",\"In R: 99.15 ÷ 99.19 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":1,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":2,\"text\":\"That's the whole loss, 2.00R. The question is the part beyond the plan: 99.15.\"},{\"value\":99.15,\"text\":\"That's in vUSD. Divide by your 1R, 99.19.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: 1.00R of slippage. A 1R plan became a 2R loss.\"},\"after\":\"Journal both numbers for each event type: the slippage in vUSD and in R. Over a few months they show what news really costs you.\",\"concept\":\"news-liquidity\"}],\"check\":[\"q-2.10.3-1\",\"q-2.10.3-2\",\"q-2.10.3-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Around a release, spreads widen, depth thins and price jumps; liquidity usually returns within minutes.\",\"A stop fills at the first bid after a jump: 96.80 instead of 98.00 cost 0.57R extra.\",\"A widened spread can trigger your stop even though the mid price never reached it.\"],\"next\":\"Next, 2.10.4: the US jobs report, the first of three big releases you'll learn to read.\"}},\"2.10.4\":{\"unit\":\"2.10\",\"courseId\":\"2.10.4\",\"bridge\":\"With the surprise from 2.10.1 and the release-minute risks from 2.10.3, you can read a real report. Here it's the US jobs report: three headline numbers, revisions, and sampling noise big enough to swallow a beat.\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"Payrolls come in 90,000 above consensus. The report's own margin of error on a monthly change is typically over 100,000 jobs. What is this surprise?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"strong\",\"label\":\"A strong, reliable signal\",\"feedback\":\"It looks strong, but it's smaller than the report's own margin of error. Statistically, it could be zero.\"},{\"id\":\"noise\",\"label\":\"Inside the noise, but the market will still react\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Statistically weak, yet price still trades the headline.\"},{\"id\":\"nothing\",\"label\":\"Nothing will happen\",\"feedback\":\"Markets trade the headline even when it's noisy. Price will likely jump, then reassess.\"}],\"answer\":\"noise\"},\"compare\":\"You chose {yours}. A 90,000 beat sits inside the report's sampling noise, yet the market trades the headline, then reassesses as the details and revisions sink in.\",\"concept\":\"nonfarm-payrolls\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"warning\",\"text\":\"Risk first: payrolls is one of the month's biggest volatility events, with wide spreads and fast jumps. Its numbers often disagree with each other, so price can whipsaw both ways. Default policy: read it, don't trade it.\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"The report\",\"text\":\"The US jobs report, [[nfp|NFP]], usually comes out on the first Friday of the month at 08:30 New York time: 15:30 in Bucharest. It has three headline numbers: payrolls, from a survey of firms; the unemployment rate, from a survey of households; and average hourly earnings. Each month's payrolls are revised in the next two reports.\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Three numbers and the revisions\",\"prompt\":\"Match each part of the jobs report to what it tells you.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"Payrolls\",\"right\":\"Jobs added by firms last month\",\"wrong\":\"Payrolls count jobs on firms' books: the headline number.\"},{\"left\":\"Unemployment rate\",\"right\":\"Share of the labour force without a job\",\"wrong\":\"The unemployment rate comes from the household survey, not from firms.\"},{\"left\":\"Average hourly earnings\",\"right\":\"Wage growth, a link to inflation\",\"wrong\":\"Earnings track pay per hour. Fast wage growth can feed inflation.\"},{\"left\":\"Revisions\",\"right\":\"Corrections to the two previous months\",\"wrong\":\"Revisions update earlier months as more survey answers come in.\"}],\"columns\":[\"Part\",\"What it tells you\"],\"correct\":\"Right. Four pieces, and they often point different ways.\",\"concept\":\"nonfarm-payrolls\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Sampling noise\",\"text\":\"Payrolls come from a survey, so they carry sampling error. The margin of error on the monthly change is typically over 100,000 jobs; for the household survey it's several hundred thousand. A 90,000 beat can't be told apart from no beat at all. The market trades it anyway, then reassesses as revisions arrive.\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Hot, cold or mixed?\",\"lines\":[\"Payrolls: 90,000 above consensus. Hot.\",\"Unemployment rate: 0.1 points above consensus. Cold.\",\"Hourly earnings: 0.1% below consensus. Cool.\",\"Revisions to the two prior months: −50,000. Cold.\",\"How many of the four readings point hot?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":1,\"tolerance\":0,\"feedback\":[{\"value\":4,\"text\":\"Only the headline beats. A higher unemployment rate, softer wages and negative revisions all point cold.\"},{\"value\":2,\"text\":\"Check the unemployment rate: higher than expected is cold. Only payrolls point hot.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: one of four.\"},\"after\":\"One hot, three cold or cool: the report is **mixed**. The headline says strong; the details say soft. Expect two waves, not one clean move.\",\"concept\":\"nonfarm-payrolls\"},{\"kind\":\"reveal\",\"title\":\"Two waves\",\"prompt\":\"LABX, market focus on growth. This mixed report lands at T0. What happens over the next five minutes?\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":100,\"h\":100.06,\"l\":99.94,\"c\":100.02},{\"o\":100.02,\"h\":100.08,\"l\":99.96,\"c\":99.98},{\"o\":99.98,\"h\":100.04,\"l\":99.92,\"c\":100},{\"o\":100,\"h\":100.06,\"l\":99.96,\"c\":100.04},{\"o\":100.04,\"h\":100.08,\"l\":99.98,\"c\":100},{\"o\":100,\"h\":100.06,\"l\":99.94,\"c\":100.02},{\"o\":100.02,\"h\":100.9,\"l\":99.98,\"c\":100.7},{\"o\":100.7,\"h\":100.8,\"l\":100.2,\"c\":100.3},{\"o\":100.3,\"h\":100.4,\"l\":99.7,\"c\":99.8},{\"o\":99.8,\"h\":99.9,\"l\":99.4,\"c\":99.5},{\"o\":99.5,\"h\":99.7,\"l\":99.36,\"c\":99.6},{\"o\":99.6,\"h\":99.66,\"l\":99.3,\"c\":99.4}],\"visible\":6},\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"rise\",\"label\":\"Keeps rising with the headline\",\"feedback\":\"The headline did lift LABX first. Then the details pulled it back.\"},{\"id\":\"fade\",\"label\":\"Spikes up, then falls below the start\",\"feedback\":\"Right.\"},{\"id\":\"none\",\"label\":\"No reaction: it's mixed\",\"feedback\":\"Mixed doesn't mean quiet. It usually means two moves, in opposite directions.\"}],\"answer\":\"fade\",\"after\":\"The headline lifted LABX to 100.90 at T0. As traders read the unemployment rate, wages and revisions, it fell to 99.30, below where it started. Stops on both sides were hit within minutes.\",\"concept\":\"nonfarm-payrolls\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Typical reactions to a hot report\",\"prompt\":\"Sort the typical first reaction to a clearly hot jobs report. Typical, not guaranteed.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"2-year government bond yield\",\"right\":\"Up\",\"wrong\":\"Strong jobs make higher rates more likely, and short-term yields track expected rates.\"},{\"left\":\"US dollar\",\"right\":\"Up\",\"wrong\":\"Higher expected rates tend to draw money into the currency.\"},{\"left\":\"Gold\",\"right\":\"Down\",\"wrong\":\"Gold pays no interest, so higher expected rates tend to weigh on it.\"},{\"left\":\"Stocks\",\"right\":\"It depends\",\"wrong\":\"Strong jobs help earnings but raise rates. Which wins depends on the market's main fear.\"}],\"columns\":[\"Market\",\"Typical move\"],\"correct\":\"Right. Typical moves, not promises: the regime decides how strong they are.\",\"concept\":\"nonfarm-payrolls\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"key\",\"text\":\"Read it. Don't trade it yet.\"}],\"task\":{\"widget\":\"bias-spotter\",\"data\":{\"options\":[{\"id\":\"hot\",\"label\":\"Clearly hot\"},{\"id\":\"cold\",\"label\":\"Clearly cold\"},{\"id\":\"mixed\",\"label\":\"Mixed\"}],\"cards\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"text\":\"Payrolls **+120,000** vs consensus · unemployment rate **0.2 points below** · hourly earnings **0.2% above** · revisions **+40,000**\",\"answer\":\"hot\",\"correct\":\"Clearly hot: more jobs, lower unemployment, faster wages and upward revisions.\",\"rationales\":{\"cold\":\"Clearly cold needs every reading pointing cold. Here all four point hot: more jobs, lower unemployment, faster wages and upward revisions.\",\"mixed\":\"Mixed is for readings that disagree. Here all four point hot: more jobs, lower unemployment, faster wages and upward revisions.\"}},{\"id\":\"b\",\"text\":\"Payrolls **−80,000** vs consensus · unemployment rate **0.1 points above** · hourly earnings **0.1% below** · revisions **−30,000**\",\"answer\":\"cold\",\"correct\":\"Clearly cold: fewer jobs, higher unemployment, softer wages and downward revisions.\",\"rationales\":{\"hot\":\"Clearly hot needs every reading pointing hot. Here all four point cold.\",\"mixed\":\"Mixed is for readings that disagree. Here all four point cold.\"}},{\"id\":\"c\",\"text\":\"Payrolls **+90,000** vs consensus · unemployment rate **0.1 points above** · hourly earnings **0.1% below** · revisions **−50,000**\",\"answer\":\"mixed\",\"correct\":\"Mixed. Only the headline is hot; the other three point cold.\",\"rationales\":{\"hot\":\"Clearly hot needs every reading pointing hot. Here only payrolls point hot; the other three point cold.\",\"cold\":\"Clearly cold needs every reading pointing cold. Here only payrolls point hot; the other three point cold.\"}},{\"id\":\"d\",\"text\":\"Payrolls **−40,000** vs consensus · unemployment rate **0.1 points below** · hourly earnings **0.2% above** · revisions **+20,000**\",\"answer\":\"mixed\",\"correct\":\"Mixed. The headline is cold, but the other three point hot.\",\"rationales\":{\"hot\":\"Clearly hot needs every reading pointing hot. Here the headline is cold but the other three point hot.\",\"cold\":\"Clearly cold needs every reading pointing cold. Here the headline is cold but the other three point hot.\"}},{\"id\":\"e\",\"text\":\"Payrolls **+60,000** vs consensus · unemployment rate **0.1 points below** · hourly earnings **0.1% above** · revisions **−70,000**\",\"answer\":\"mixed\",\"correct\":\"Mixed. Three readings are hot, but the revisions take back more jobs than the headline added.\",\"rationales\":{\"hot\":\"Clearly hot needs every reading pointing hot. Here three readings point hot, but the revisions take back more jobs than the headline added.\",\"cold\":\"Clearly cold needs every reading pointing cold. Here three readings point hot, but the revisions take back more jobs than the headline added.\"}}]},\"goal\":\"Five synthetic jobs reports. Classify each as clearly hot, clearly cold or mixed. Clear only when all three numbers and the revisions point the same way.\",\"success\":{\"field\":\"solved\",\"eq\":5},\"correct\":\"All five classified. Two were clear, three mixed: mixed is common, and it's when whipsaws are most likely.\",\"hints\":[\"Lower unemployment is hot. Downward revisions are cold.\",\"One reading against the rest makes the report mixed.\"],\"showMe\":{\"solved\":5},\"concept\":\"nonfarm-payrolls\"},\"check\":[\"q-2.10.4-1\",\"q-2.10.4-2\",\"q-2.10.4-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"The jobs report: payrolls, the unemployment rate and hourly earnings, from two surveys, plus revisions.\",\"A beat smaller than the report's margin of error is noise, even when price still jumps.\",\"Clearly hot or cold only when all three numbers and revisions agree; otherwise it's mixed.\"],\"next\":\"Next, 2.10.5: CPI, the inflation report, where headline and core can pull price two ways.\"}},\"2.10.5\":{\"unit\":\"2.10\",\"courseId\":\"2.10.5\",\"bridge\":\"In 2.10.4 a mixed jobs report moved in two waves. Inflation data does the same when headline and core disagree. Here you size a CPI surprise as z, as in 2.10.1, and wait for core before you decide.\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"Headline CPI comes in 0.2 above consensus on energy prices. Core comes in 0.1 below. LABX's first spike is down. What next?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"fall\",\"label\":\"Keeps falling\"},{\"id\":\"reverse\",\"label\":\"Reverses as core is digested\"},{\"id\":\"nothing\",\"label\":\"Nothing more happens\"},{\"id\":\"cant\",\"label\":\"Can't tell\"}],\"answer\":\"reverse\"},\"compare\":\"You chose {yours}. When headline and core disagree, the second wave usually follows core, so a reversal is the likely path. Likely, not certain.\",\"concept\":\"cpi-inflation\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"warning\",\"text\":\"Risk first: index futures can gap at the CPI minute, and the reaction often comes in two waves. The first follows the headline, the second follows core. A stop hit in the first wave can miss the turn in the second.\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"What CPI measures\",\"text\":\"The [[cpi]] tracks prices paid by consumers, from tens of thousands of price quotes each month. It typically comes out at 08:30 New York time. Headline CPI covers all items. [[core-inflation|Core]] leaves out food and energy, the most volatile parts. Markets focus on core's month-on-month change, quoted to one decimal.\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"Same release, two stories\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"bars\",\"bars\":[{\"label\":\"Headline, consensus\",\"value\":0.3,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"label\":\"Headline, actual\",\"value\":0.5,\"role\":\"down\"},{\"label\":\"Core, consensus\",\"value\":0.3,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"label\":\"Core, actual\",\"value\":0.2,\"role\":\"up\"}],\"unit\":\"%\",\"steps\":[\"Headline, all items: 0.5% against 0.3% expected. Energy did most of it.\",\"Core, without food and energy: 0.2% against 0.3%. Underlying prices cooled.\"]},\"text\":\"Energy swings from month to month; core shows the underlying trend that interest rates respond to. That's why the second wave usually follows core.\",\"concept\":\"cpi-inflation\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Sizing a CPI surprise\",\"lines\":[\"Core CPI, month on month: consensus 0.3%, actual 0.2%.\",\"Surprise: 0.2 − 0.3 = −0.1 points.\",\"Core surprises usually vary by about 0.1 points: σ = 0.1.\",\"z = −0.1 ÷ 0.1 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":-1,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"feedback\":[{\"value\":1,\"text\":\"Check the sign: core came in below consensus.\"},{\"value\":-0.1,\"text\":\"That's the surprise in points. Divide by σ, 0.1.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: z = −1.0.\"},\"after\":\"A full standard deviation. For a number quoted to one decimal, a 0.1 miss in core is typically already a large surprise.\",\"concept\":\"cpi-inflation\"},{\"kind\":\"reveal\",\"title\":\"Two waves\",\"prompt\":\"Market focus: inflation. The hot headline hits LABX first. What happens over the next five minutes?\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":100,\"h\":100.06,\"l\":99.94,\"c\":100.02},{\"o\":100.02,\"h\":100.08,\"l\":99.96,\"c\":99.98},{\"o\":99.98,\"h\":100.04,\"l\":99.92,\"c\":100},{\"o\":100,\"h\":100.06,\"l\":99.96,\"c\":100.04},{\"o\":100.04,\"h\":100.08,\"l\":99.98,\"c\":100},{\"o\":100,\"h\":100.06,\"l\":99.94,\"c\":100.02},{\"o\":100.02,\"h\":100.06,\"l\":99.2,\"c\":99.3},{\"o\":99.3,\"h\":99.7,\"l\":99.24,\"c\":99.6},{\"o\":99.6,\"h\":100.1,\"l\":99.5,\"c\":100},{\"o\":100,\"h\":100.5,\"l\":99.9,\"c\":100.4},{\"o\":100.4,\"h\":100.7,\"l\":100.3,\"c\":100.6},{\"o\":100.6,\"h\":100.76,\"l\":100.44,\"c\":100.7}],\"visible\":6},\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"fall\",\"label\":\"Keeps falling with the headline\",\"feedback\":\"The first wave did follow the headline. Then core took over.\"},{\"id\":\"reverse\",\"label\":\"Falls, then climbs back above the start\",\"feedback\":\"Right.\"},{\"id\":\"none\",\"label\":\"No more movement\",\"feedback\":\"Contradictory details rarely leave price still. Expect a second wave.\"}],\"answer\":\"reverse\",\"after\":\"First wave, the headline: down to 99.20. Second wave, core: back above the pre-release price within minutes, closing at 100.70. A tight stop under the first spike's start filled near the low, just before the turn.\",\"concept\":\"cpi-inflation\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Typical reactions\",\"prompt\":\"Sort the typical first reaction to each CPI reading. Typical, and dependent on the regime.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"Hot CPI: bond yields\",\"right\":\"Up\",\"wrong\":\"Hotter inflation makes higher rates more likely, so yields tend to rise.\"},{\"left\":\"Hot CPI: the dollar\",\"right\":\"Up\",\"wrong\":\"Higher expected rates tend to support the currency.\"},{\"left\":\"Hot CPI: growth stocks\",\"right\":\"Down\",\"wrong\":\"Growth stocks are valued on distant profits, which higher rates discount more.\"},{\"left\":\"Cool CPI: bond yields\",\"right\":\"Down\",\"wrong\":\"Cooler inflation makes cuts more likely, so yields tend to fall.\"},{\"left\":\"Cool CPI: gold\",\"right\":\"Up\",\"wrong\":\"Lower expected rates make a metal that pays no interest more attractive.\"}],\"columns\":[\"Reading\",\"Typical move\"],\"correct\":\"Right. Typical first moves; the regime decides how strong they are.\",\"concept\":\"cpi-inflation\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"key\",\"text\":\"Core first, then decide.\"}],\"task\":{\"widget\":\"bias-spotter\",\"data\":{\"options\":[{\"id\":\"flat5\",\"label\":\"They disagree: flat until T+5\"},{\"id\":\"agree\",\"label\":\"They agree: skip the first minute, then follow your plan\"},{\"id\":\"now\",\"label\":\"Trade the first spike now\"}],\"cards\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"text\":\"Headline **0.2 above** consensus · core **0.1 below**\",\"answer\":\"flat5\",\"correct\":\"They disagree: flat until T+5. The second wave will likely follow core.\",\"rationales\":{\"agree\":\"Following the plan after the first minute is for releases where both point the same way. Here headline is hot and core is cool: they disagree.\",\"now\":\"Trading the first spike breaks the rule at every release: the first minute has the widest spreads. Here headline is hot and core is cool: they disagree.\"}},{\"id\":\"b\",\"text\":\"Headline **0.1 above** consensus · core **0.2 above**\",\"answer\":\"agree\",\"correct\":\"They agree, both hot. Skip the first minute, then follow your plan.\",\"rationales\":{\"flat5\":\"Flat until T+5 is for releases where headline and core point opposite ways. Here both are hot: they agree.\",\"now\":\"Trading the first spike breaks the rule at every release: the first minute has the widest spreads. Here both are hot: they agree.\"}},{\"id\":\"c\",\"text\":\"Headline **0.2 below** consensus, on falling energy prices · core **0.1 above**\",\"answer\":\"flat5\",\"correct\":\"They disagree: cheap energy pulled the headline down while core ran hot. Flat until T+5.\",\"rationales\":{\"agree\":\"Following the plan after the first minute is for releases where both point the same way. Here headline is cool and core is hot: they disagree.\",\"now\":\"Trading the first spike breaks the rule at every release: the first minute has the widest spreads. Here headline is cool and core is hot: they disagree.\"}},{\"id\":\"d\",\"text\":\"Headline **0.1 below** consensus · core **0.1 below**\",\"answer\":\"agree\",\"correct\":\"They agree, both cool. Skip the first minute, then follow your plan.\",\"rationales\":{\"flat5\":\"Flat until T+5 is for releases where headline and core point opposite ways. Here both are cool: they agree.\",\"now\":\"Trading the first spike breaks the rule at every release: the first minute has the widest spreads. Here both are cool: they agree.\"}},{\"id\":\"e\",\"text\":\"Headline **0.1 above** consensus · core **0.2 below**\",\"answer\":\"flat5\",\"correct\":\"They disagree: flat until T+5.\",\"rationales\":{\"agree\":\"Following the plan after the first minute is for releases where both point the same way. Here headline is hot and core is cool: they disagree.\",\"now\":\"Trading the first spike breaks the rule at every release: the first minute has the widest spreads. Here headline is hot and core is cool: they disagree.\"}}]},\"goal\":\"Your rule: if headline and core disagree, stay flat until 5 minutes after the release; never act in the first minute. Apply it to five synthetic CPI releases.\",\"success\":{\"field\":\"solved\",\"eq\":5},\"correct\":\"Rule kept at all five. Three releases disagreed: the second wave decides those, and waiting costs only the first, most expensive minutes.\",\"hints\":[\"Above consensus is hot, below is cool. Compare the two signs.\",\"Opposite signs mean flat until T+5. No release gets traded in its first minute.\"],\"showMe\":{\"solved\":5},\"concept\":\"cpi-inflation\"},\"check\":[\"q-2.10.5-1\",\"q-2.10.5-2\",\"q-2.10.5-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Core CPI leaves out food and energy; markets watch core month-on-month because it shows the trend.\",\"Size the surprise by the usual spread: a 0.1 core miss with σ 0.1 is z = −1.0.\",\"When headline and core disagree, stay flat until the second wave shows which one wins.\"],\"next\":\"Next, 2.10.6: central banks, whose rate decisions respond to the inflation you just learned to read.\"}},\"2.10.6\":{\"unit\":\"2.10\",\"courseId\":\"2.10.6\",\"bridge\":\"In 2.10.5 inflation data moved prices through what it implied for interest rates. Here are the banks that set those rates. Their decisions surprise like any release (2.10.1), with the spreads and slippage of 2.10.3, at the statement and again at the press conference.\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"The market prices a 70% chance of a 25 bp rate cut. Which outcome is the bigger surprise: the cut, or no change?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"cut\",\"label\":\"The cut\",\"feedback\":\"The cut is the likely outcome, so most of it is already in the price. A hold surprises more.\"},{\"id\":\"hold\",\"label\":\"No change\",\"feedback\":\"Right. A hold is far from what's priced: 17.5 bp hawkish, against 7.5 bp dovish for the cut.\"}],\"answer\":\"hold\"},\"compare\":\"You chose {yours}. The market expected a 17.5 bp cut on average. A cut is a 7.5 bp dovish surprise; no change is a 17.5 bp hawkish one.\",\"concept\":\"central-bank-decisions\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"warning\",\"text\":\"Risk first: price can gap at the decision minute. A second move often comes with the press conference, with 30 to 60 minutes of high volatility, and the whipsaw can run from statement to conference to later speeches.\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"What a central bank decides\",\"text\":\"A [[central-bank]] sets its policy rate at scheduled meetings: the [[rate-decision]]. Most aim at stable prices, typically a 2% inflation target; the Fed also aims at maximum employment. Long before the meeting, futures prices show what the market expects. As with any release, the decision moves price by how far it differs from that.\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"When they decide\",\"text\":\"Typical schedules: the Fed, the ECB, the Bank of England and the Bank of Japan each meet eight times a year. The Fed's statement lands at 14:00 New York time, usually 21:00 in Bucharest, with a press conference 30 minutes later. The ECB decides at 14:15 Frankfurt time, 15:15 here; the Bank of England at 12:00 London, 14:00 here.\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"A surprise from probabilities\",\"lines\":[\"Futures price a 70% chance of a 25 bp cut, and 30% of no change.\",\"Expected change: 0.70 × 25 = 17.5 bp lower.\",\"If they cut 25: 25 − 17.5 = 7.5 bp more [[dovish]] than priced.\",\"If they hold: 0 against an expected 17.5 bp cut. Size of the [[hawkish]] surprise, in bp = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":17.5,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"bp\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":7.5,\"text\":\"That's the surprise if they cut. A hold delivers 0 against 17.5 expected.\"},{\"value\":25,\"text\":\"The market didn't expect a full 25 bp: only 70% of it, 17.5.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: 17.5 bp, more than twice the cut's surprise.\"},\"after\":\"'No change' sounds like no news, yet here it's the big surprise. Compare a decision with what was priced, not with the current rate. A [[basis-point]] is 0.01 percentage points.\",\"concept\":\"central-bank-decisions\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Hawk or dove?\",\"prompt\":\"Sort each line from a policy statement.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"'Further policy firming may be appropriate.'\",\"right\":\"Hawkish\",\"wrong\":\"'Firming' means tightening: higher rates.\"},{\"left\":\"'The Committee will be patient.'\",\"right\":\"Dovish\",\"wrong\":\"Patience means no hurry to raise rates.\"},{\"left\":\"'Risks to inflation remain tilted to the upside.'\",\"right\":\"Hawkish\",\"wrong\":\"Worry about inflation points to tighter policy.\"},{\"left\":\"'Policy will stay supportive until the recovery is secure.'\",\"right\":\"Dovish\",\"wrong\":\"'Supportive' means low rates, kept for longer.\"}],\"columns\":[\"Statement line\",\"Tone\"],\"correct\":\"Right. But tone moves price only against what was expected: a hawkish line that's already priced is no surprise.\",\"concept\":\"central-bank-decisions\"},{\"kind\":\"reveal\",\"title\":\"Statement, then conference\",\"prompt\":\"Five-minute candles. The decision matches expectations exactly and LABX barely moves. The press conference starts 30 minutes later. What next?\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":100,\"h\":100.08,\"l\":99.94,\"c\":100.04},{\"o\":100.04,\"h\":100.1,\"l\":99.98,\"c\":100.02},{\"o\":100.02,\"h\":100.06,\"l\":99.96,\"c\":99.98},{\"o\":99.98,\"h\":100.04,\"l\":99.92,\"c\":100},{\"o\":100,\"h\":100.06,\"l\":99.94,\"c\":100.02},{\"o\":100.02,\"h\":100.08,\"l\":99.98,\"c\":100},{\"o\":100,\"h\":100.3,\"l\":99.8,\"c\":100.1},{\"o\":100.1,\"h\":100.2,\"l\":99.96,\"c\":100.04},{\"o\":100.04,\"h\":100.14,\"l\":99.94,\"c\":100.08},{\"o\":100.08,\"h\":100.16,\"l\":99.98,\"c\":100.06},{\"o\":100.06,\"h\":100.12,\"l\":99.96,\"c\":100},{\"o\":100,\"h\":100.1,\"l\":99.92,\"c\":99.98},{\"o\":99.98,\"h\":100.04,\"l\":99.1,\"c\":99.2},{\"o\":99.2,\"h\":99.4,\"l\":98.9,\"c\":99},{\"o\":99,\"h\":99.24,\"l\":98.8,\"c\":98.9}],\"visible\":9,\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":6,\"at\":\"high\",\"label\":\"STATEMENT\",\"role\":\"neutral\"}]},\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"quiet\",\"label\":\"Quiet: the decision was as expected\",\"feedback\":\"The decision was. The press conference added news of its own.\"},{\"id\":\"conf\",\"label\":\"A bigger move at the press conference\",\"feedback\":\"Right.\"},{\"id\":\"rally\",\"label\":\"A rally once the statement is digested\",\"feedback\":\"The statement held no surprise to digest. The news came from the press conference.\"}],\"answer\":\"conf\",\"after\":\"The statement moved LABX 0.30 at most. At the press conference the chair hinted at fewer cuts than priced: a hawkish surprise. LABX fell 1.08 in the next 15 minutes.\",\"concept\":\"central-bank-decisions\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"A hike, and the currency falls?\",\"prompt\":\"A central bank raises rates for the first time in years. The hike was fully priced, and the bank says further hikes will be slow. What does its currency do?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"up\",\"label\":\"Rises: rates went up\",\"feedback\":\"Rates did rise, but that was priced. The news was the slow path ahead, dovish against expectations.\"},{\"id\":\"down\",\"label\":\"Falls: the guidance was more dovish than priced\",\"feedback\":\"Right.\"},{\"id\":\"flat\",\"label\":\"Nothing: the hike was expected\",\"feedback\":\"The hike alone might have done little. The slow-path guidance added a dovish surprise.\"}],\"answer\":\"down\"},\"reveal\":\"It can fall. The hike was in the price; the news was a slower path than expected. Something like this happened to the yen in 2024: the Bank of Japan's first hike in 17 years, and the yen still weakened past 150 per dollar.\",\"concept\":\"central-bank-decisions\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"key\",\"text\":\"To lift its currency, a central bank must be more hawkish than priced.\"}],\"task\":{\"widget\":\"bias-spotter\",\"data\":{\"options\":[{\"id\":\"hawk\",\"label\":\"More hawkish than priced\"},{\"id\":\"asis\",\"label\":\"As priced\"},{\"id\":\"dove\",\"label\":\"More dovish than priced\"}],\"cards\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"text\":\"**Priced:** one more hike, then a pause. **Statement:** 'We will raise rates further and keep them high for longer.'\",\"answer\":\"hawk\",\"correct\":\"More hawkish. 'Further' and 'for longer' go beyond one hike and a pause.\",\"rationales\":{\"asis\":\"As priced means it matches what the market expected. Here 'further and for longer' goes beyond one hike and a pause.\",\"dove\":\"More dovish means looser than the market expected. Here 'further and for longer' goes beyond one hike and a pause.\"}},{\"id\":\"b\",\"text\":\"**Priced:** a cut at this meeting, 90%. **Decision:** a cut, and 'further easing is likely.'\",\"answer\":\"dove\",\"correct\":\"More dovish. The cut was priced; the promise of more cuts wasn't.\",\"rationales\":{\"hawk\":\"More hawkish means tighter than the market expected. Here the cut was priced, but 'further easing' adds cuts the market hadn't priced.\",\"asis\":\"As priced means it matches what the market expected. Here the cut was priced, but 'further easing' adds cuts the market hadn't priced.\"}},{\"id\":\"c\",\"text\":\"**Priced:** no change. **Decision:** no change, and 'policy is well positioned.'\",\"answer\":\"asis\",\"correct\":\"As priced. Nothing new, so little to move price.\",\"rationales\":{\"hawk\":\"More hawkish means tighter than the market expected. Here both the decision and the tone match what was priced.\",\"dove\":\"More dovish means looser than the market expected. Here both the decision and the tone match what was priced.\"}},{\"id\":\"d\",\"text\":\"**Priced:** three cuts this year. **Statement:** 'We see little need to cut further this year.'\",\"answer\":\"hawk\",\"correct\":\"More hawkish: fewer cuts than the three priced.\",\"rationales\":{\"asis\":\"As priced means it matches what the market expected. Here the bank signals fewer cuts than the three priced.\",\"dove\":\"More dovish means looser than the market expected. Here the bank signals fewer cuts than the three priced.\"}},{\"id\":\"e\",\"text\":\"**Priced:** a hike, 80%. **Decision:** no change; 'we need more data.'\",\"answer\":\"dove\",\"correct\":\"More dovish. The likely hike didn't come.\",\"rationales\":{\"hawk\":\"More hawkish means tighter than the market expected. Here an 80% likely hike didn't come.\",\"asis\":\"As priced means it matches what the market expected. Here an 80% likely hike didn't come.\"}},{\"id\":\"f\",\"text\":\"**Priced:** no change now, cuts later in the year. **Statement:** 'Progress on inflation has stalled; cuts are not on the table.'\",\"answer\":\"hawk\",\"correct\":\"More hawkish. The decision matched, but the cuts priced for later were taken away.\",\"rationales\":{\"asis\":\"As priced means it matches what the market expected. Here the cuts priced for later are taken off the table.\",\"dove\":\"More dovish means looser than the market expected. Here the cuts priced for later are taken off the table.\"}}]},\"goal\":\"Six decisions, each read against what the market priced. Is each one more hawkish than priced, as priced, or more dovish?\",\"success\":{\"field\":\"solved\",\"eq\":6},\"correct\":\"All six placed. Tone is relative: a cut can read hawkish and no change dovish, depending on what was priced.\",\"hints\":[\"First read what was priced. Then ask: is the decision or the guidance tighter or looser than that?\",\"A priced move is no surprise. Count only what's new.\"],\"showMe\":{\"solved\":6},\"concept\":\"central-bank-decisions\"},\"check\":[\"q-2.10.6-1\",\"q-2.10.6-2\",\"q-2.10.6-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Central banks set rates at scheduled meetings; prices move on the gap between decision and what was priced.\",\"Expected move = probability × size: 70% of 25 bp is 17.5 bp, so a hold surprises more.\",\"Hawkish and dovish only mean something against expectations. Watch the press conference as well as the decision.\"],\"next\":\"Next, 2.10.7: your news policy, stand aside, reduce or trade the surprise, decided before the event.\"}},\"2.10.7\":{\"unit\":\"2.10\",\"courseId\":\"2.10.7\",\"bridge\":\"In 2.10.4 to 2.10.6 you read three big releases and saw how they jump. Here you decide in advance what to do about them: stand aside, reduce to a size that survives a gap, as you did for weekends in 2.9.8, or trade the reaction.\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"You hold 47 LABX long, risking 98.66. A red event is 20 minutes away. Hold, reduce, or go flat?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"hold\",\"label\":\"Hold\"},{\"id\":\"reduce\",\"label\":\"Reduce\"},{\"id\":\"flat\",\"label\":\"Go flat\"}]},\"compare\":\"You chose {yours}. A written policy decides this before the week starts. Flat by T−15 is the default; reducing works only down to a size a gap can't push past 1R. Holding 47 is neither.\",\"concept\":\"news-policy\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"warning\",\"text\":\"Risk first, recapped: spreads widen many times over, stops fill after the jump, and a gap can skip your stop entirely. And no timing rule protects you from an unscheduled shock.\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Three policies\",\"text\":\"A **news policy** picks one of three, before the event. **Stand aside**: no new orders from T−15, and flat, or as the plan says. **Reduce**: keep a smaller position, sized so a gap stays within 1R. **Trade the reaction**: act only after the number. The default is to [[stand-aside|stand aside]].\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Which policy when?\",\"prompt\":\"Match each policy to the situation it fits.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"Stand aside\",\"right\":\"Any red event on your instrument: the default\",\"wrong\":\"Standing aside is the default for red events. It costs nothing but a missed move.\"},{\"left\":\"Reduce\",\"right\":\"A swing position you want to keep through the event\",\"wrong\":\"Reducing is for a position you mean to keep: cut it to a size a gap can't push past 1R.\"},{\"left\":\"Trade the reaction\",\"right\":\"After the number only: a top release, |z| over 2, details agreeing\",\"wrong\":\"Trading the reaction waits for the number and needs a big, clean surprise.\"}],\"columns\":[\"Policy\",\"When it fits\"],\"correct\":\"Right. Three policies, each chosen before the event, never at T−1 minute.\",\"concept\":\"news-policy\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Reduce: size for the gap\",\"lines\":[\"Long from 100.00, stop 98.00. ATR 0.80.\",\"Plan for a gap of 4 × ATR = 3.20: an exit at 96.80, past your stop.\",\"Each unit loses 3.20 there, plus about 0.10 of commissions.\",\"Most whole units that keep the gap loss within 100.00, commissions included = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":30,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"units\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":31,\"text\":\"31 units lose 102.26 in the gap, commissions included: over 100.00.\"},{\"value\":47,\"text\":\"That's your current size. In the gap it would lose 155.03.\"},{\"value\":50,\"text\":\"100.00 ÷ 2.00 = 50 sizes for the stop, not for the gap.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: 30 units lose 98.96 in the gap; 31 would lose 102.26.\"},\"after\":\"Keep the stop at 98.00: there, 30 units lose only 62.97. The gap you fear sets the size, not the stop you hope fills. Gaps beyond 4 × ATR still happen: the tails are fat.\",\"concept\":\"news-policy\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"Illustration: the first minute, in 5-second bars\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"chart\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":100,\"h\":100.02,\"l\":99.98,\"c\":100},{\"o\":100,\"h\":100.8,\"l\":100,\"c\":100.7},{\"o\":100.7,\"h\":100.9,\"l\":100.66,\"c\":100.86},{\"o\":100.86,\"h\":100.94,\"l\":100.8,\"c\":100.9},{\"o\":100.9,\"h\":100.96,\"l\":100.84,\"c\":100.88},{\"o\":100.88,\"h\":100.98,\"l\":100.86,\"c\":100.94},{\"o\":100.94,\"h\":101.02,\"l\":100.9,\"c\":100.96},{\"o\":100.96,\"h\":101,\"l\":100.9,\"c\":100.94},{\"o\":100.94,\"h\":101.04,\"l\":100.92,\"c\":101},{\"o\":101,\"h\":101.06,\"l\":100.96,\"c\":100.98},{\"o\":100.98,\"h\":101.04,\"l\":100.94,\"c\":101.02},{\"o\":101.02,\"h\":101.06,\"l\":100.98,\"c\":101},{\"o\":101,\"h\":101.04,\"l\":100.96,\"c\":101}]},\"steps\":[{\"text\":\"T0: the number lands. Algorithms reprice LABX within the first second or two.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":1,\"at\":\"high\",\"label\":\"T0\",\"role\":\"neutral\"}]},{\"text\":\"A person needs seconds to read, decide and click. Here the order lands at 100.85.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":2,\"price\":100.85,\"label\":\"YOUR CLICK 100.85\",\"role\":\"accent\"}]},{\"text\":\"That's 85% of the first minute's move, 0.85 of 1.00, already gone, at the widest spread.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":101,\"role\":\"neutral\",\"label\":\"1-MIN CLOSE 101.00\"}]}]},\"concept\":\"news-policy\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Trading the surprise\",\"text\":\"The first move does tend to follow the surprise at top releases: [[evidence-grade|evidence grade]] B. But most of the repricing happens within seconds, before a person can act, and at the widest spreads. Retail news scalping rates D. Straddles, orders on both sides of the price, can fill twice in a whipsaw.\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"The same gap, three policies\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"bars\",\"bars\":[{\"label\":\"Hold 47 units\",\"value\":155.03,\"role\":\"down\"},{\"label\":\"Reduce to 30\",\"value\":98.96,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"label\":\"Stand aside\",\"value\":0,\"role\":\"up\"}],\"unit\":\" vUSD\",\"steps\":[\"Hold 47 units: a 4 × ATR gap costs 155.03, over your 100.00 budget.\",\"Reduce to 30: the same gap costs 98.96, within 1R.\",\"Stand aside: the gap costs nothing. A missed move is the only price.\"]},\"text\":\"Loss if LABX gaps 3.20 below your 100.00 entry, commissions included.\",\"concept\":\"news-policy\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"key\",\"text\":\"Standing aside is a strategy. It is the default.\"}],\"task\":{\"widget\":\"position-size\",\"goal\":\"Keep a LABX long from 100.00 through a red event. ATR is 1.00; plan for a 4 × ATR gap, so the widget's stop is 96.00. Type the most units that lose no more than 100.00 there.\",\"start\":{\"account\":10000,\"riskPct\":1,\"entry\":100,\"stop\":96,\"units\":25},\"locked\":[\"account\",\"riskPct\",\"entry\",\"stop\"],\"hidden\":[\"simpleQty\",\"qty\"],\"success\":{\"all\":[{\"field\":\"units\",\"eq\":24},{\"field\":\"stop\",\"eq\":96}]},\"feedback\":[{\"when\":{\"field\":\"units\",\"eq\":25},\"text\":\"25 units lose 100.00 on price alone. The commissions add 2.45: 102.45, over budget.\"},{\"when\":{\"field\":\"units\",\"gt\":25},\"text\":\"{units} units would lose {lossAtStop:2} in that gap, over 100.00.\"},{\"when\":{\"field\":\"units\",\"lt\":24},\"text\":\"{units} units fit, but one more would too. Find the most that does.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. 24 units lose 98.36 in a 4 × ATR gap. At a normal stop of 98.00, they'd lose just 50.38.\",\"hints\":[\"The gap loss is units × 4.00, plus both commissions.\",\"Without commissions, 25 units lose exactly 100.00. The commissions push that over.\",\"Step down one unit and check the loss.\"],\"showMe\":{\"units\":24},\"concept\":\"news-policy\"},\"check\":[\"q-2.10.7-1\",\"q-2.10.7-2\",\"q-2.10.7-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Pick the news policy before the week starts: stand aside by default, reduce, or trade only the reaction.\",\"To hold through an event, size so a k × ATR gap stays within 1R: 30 units, not 47.\",\"The first seconds belong to algorithms. You'd enter late, at the widest spread: no retail edge.\"],\"next\":\"Next, 2.10.8: the second move, how to trade the reaction once spreads have normalised.\"}},\"2.10.8\":{\"unit\":\"2.10\",\"courseId\":\"2.10.8\",\"bridge\":\"In 2.10.7 trading the reaction was the third policy. Here is how: wait for spreads to normalise, mark the post-release range and trade its break like the range breakout of 2.8.4, or pass when details disagree, as in CPI's two waves (2.10.5).\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"Two news policies: enter at the release, or wait for a break of the 5-minute post-release range. Over many events, which has the smaller worst trade?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"now\",\"label\":\"Enter at the release\",\"feedback\":\"Entering at the release meets the widest spreads and the jumps. Its worst trade can blow far past 1R.\"},{\"id\":\"wait\",\"label\":\"Wait for the range break\",\"feedback\":\"Right.\"}],\"answer\":\"wait\"},\"compare\":\"You chose {yours}. Waiting has the smaller worst trade by design: its stop is set after spreads normalise, at a known distance, sized to 1R.\",\"concept\":\"second-move\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"warning\",\"text\":\"Risk first: in the first 1 to 15 minutes, spreads stay wide, the reaction can come in two waves, and price whipsaws. A press conference can bring a bigger move later. After the news, prefer limit orders.\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"The setup\",\"text\":\"The [[second-move]] waits out the chaos. After the release, wait until the spread is back to at most 2 × normal: 0.20 on LABX. Mark the [[post-release-range]]: the high and low of the first 5 or 15 minutes. Enter on a break of it in the surprise's direction, details agreeing. Stop: the range's other side.\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"Two waves, then a range\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"chart\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":100.4,\"h\":100.48,\"l\":100.34,\"c\":100.42},{\"o\":100.42,\"h\":100.5,\"l\":100.36,\"c\":100.38},{\"o\":100.38,\"h\":100.46,\"l\":100.32,\"c\":100.44},{\"o\":100.44,\"h\":100.5,\"l\":100.38,\"c\":100.4},{\"o\":100.4,\"h\":100.46,\"l\":100.34,\"c\":100.4},{\"o\":101.3,\"h\":101.95,\"l\":101,\"c\":101.7},{\"o\":101.7,\"h\":101.8,\"l\":101.05,\"c\":101.15},{\"o\":101.15,\"h\":101.6,\"l\":101.1,\"c\":101.5},{\"o\":101.5,\"h\":102,\"l\":101.4,\"c\":101.8},{\"o\":101.8,\"h\":101.9,\"l\":101.35,\"c\":101.45},{\"o\":101.45,\"h\":101.85,\"l\":101.3,\"c\":101.75},{\"o\":101.75,\"h\":102.35,\"l\":101.7,\"c\":102.25},{\"o\":102.25,\"h\":102.7,\"l\":102.15,\"c\":102.6},{\"o\":102.6,\"h\":102.8,\"l\":102.4,\"c\":102.7}]},\"steps\":[{\"text\":\"T0: price jumps through an empty book. Spread 1.00, ten times normal.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":5,\"at\":\"high\",\"label\":\"SPREAD 1.00\",\"role\":\"down\"}]},{\"text\":\"T+1: the second wave runs back down. Stops on both sides get hit.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":6,\"at\":\"low\",\"label\":\"0.60\",\"role\":\"down\"}]},{\"text\":\"As spreads settle, the first five minutes define the range: 101.00 to 102.00.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"zone\",\"from\":101,\"to\":102,\"role\":\"neutral\",\"label\":\"POST-RELEASE RANGE\"}]},{\"text\":\"T+6: price breaks the range high, in the surprise's direction.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":11,\"at\":\"high\",\"label\":\"BREAK\",\"role\":\"accent\"}]}]},\"concept\":\"second-move\"},{\"kind\":\"hotspot\",\"title\":\"When can you act?\",\"prompt\":\"Normal spread is 0.10. Each label shows that minute's spread. Tap the first candle where the spread is back under 2 × normal.\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":100.4,\"h\":100.48,\"l\":100.34,\"c\":100.42},{\"o\":100.42,\"h\":100.5,\"l\":100.36,\"c\":100.38},{\"o\":100.38,\"h\":100.46,\"l\":100.32,\"c\":100.44},{\"o\":100.44,\"h\":100.5,\"l\":100.38,\"c\":100.4},{\"o\":100.4,\"h\":100.46,\"l\":100.34,\"c\":100.4},{\"o\":101.3,\"h\":101.95,\"l\":101,\"c\":101.7},{\"o\":101.7,\"h\":101.8,\"l\":101.05,\"c\":101.15},{\"o\":101.15,\"h\":101.6,\"l\":101.1,\"c\":101.5},{\"o\":101.5,\"h\":102,\"l\":101.4,\"c\":101.8},{\"o\":101.8,\"h\":101.9,\"l\":101.35,\"c\":101.45},{\"o\":101.45,\"h\":101.85,\"l\":101.3,\"c\":101.75}],\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":5,\"at\":\"high\",\"label\":\"1.00\",\"role\":\"down\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":6,\"at\":\"high\",\"label\":\"0.60\",\"role\":\"down\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":7,\"at\":\"high\",\"label\":\"0.40\",\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":8,\"at\":\"high\",\"label\":\"0.25\",\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":9,\"at\":\"high\",\"label\":\"0.18\",\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":10,\"at\":\"high\",\"label\":\"0.12\",\"role\":\"neutral\"}]},\"zones\":[{\"id\":\"tp2\",\"label\":\"T+2\",\"index\":7,\"feedback\":\"0.40 is four times normal. Wait.\"},{\"id\":\"tp3\",\"label\":\"T+3\",\"index\":8,\"feedback\":\"0.25 is still over 0.20. One more minute.\"},{\"id\":\"tp4\",\"label\":\"T+4\",\"index\":9},{\"id\":\"tp5\",\"label\":\"T+5\",\"index\":10,\"feedback\":\"0.12 is fine, but the spread was already under 0.20 a minute earlier.\"}],\"answer\":\"tp4\",\"correct\":\"Right: T+4, spread 0.18, under 0.20. Before that, every order paid a news-sized spread.\",\"concept\":\"second-move\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Size the break\",\"lines\":[\"Range: 101.00–102.00. The spread is back to 0.10.\",\"Buy the break at 102.05; stop 100.95, below the range: 1.10 a unit.\",\"Round-trip cost ≈ 0.20 a unit: 0.20 ÷ 1.10 = 0.18R. Acceptable.\",\"Most whole units with the loss at the stop, commissions included, within 100.00 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":83,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"units\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":90,\"text\":\"100.00 ÷ 1.10 = 90 leaves out the commissions. 90 units would lose 108.15.\"},{\"value\":84,\"text\":\"84 units lose 100.93 with commissions: just over.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: 83 units lose 99.73 at the stop.\"},\"after\":\"Target at 2R: 102.05 + 2 × 1.10 = 104.25. A false break costs 1R at a normal spread, not a gap at a news spread.\",\"concept\":\"second-move\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Where it fails\",\"prompt\":\"Match each way the second move fails to the sign that warns you.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"Conflicting details\",\"right\":\"Headline and core, or payrolls and wages, point opposite ways\",\"wrong\":\"Conflicting details show up inside the report itself, as numbers pointing opposite ways.\"},{\"left\":\"A small surprise\",\"right\":\"|z| under 1: breaks of the range tend to fall back in\",\"wrong\":\"A small surprise gives the break no fuel, so it often falls back into the range.\"},{\"left\":\"The press conference\",\"right\":\"A scheduled conference 30 minutes after a rate decision\",\"wrong\":\"A conference after a decision can bring a bigger move the other way.\"},{\"left\":\"Spreads still wide\",\"right\":\"The break comes while the spread is over 2 × normal\",\"wrong\":\"If the break comes before spreads normalise, the rule says wait.\"}],\"columns\":[\"Failure\",\"Warning sign\"],\"correct\":\"Right. Any one of these, and the plan says pass.\",\"concept\":\"second-move\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"info\",\"text\":\"Evidence: waiting for normal spreads is a sound way to cut risk (grade B). As a source of edge, the continuation is plausible but less documented (grade C). Test it in your own journal before you trust it.\"}],\"task\":{\"widget\":\"bracket-builder\",\"data\":{\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":100.4,\"h\":100.48,\"l\":100.34,\"c\":100.42},{\"o\":100.42,\"h\":100.5,\"l\":100.36,\"c\":100.38},{\"o\":100.38,\"h\":100.46,\"l\":100.32,\"c\":100.44},{\"o\":100.44,\"h\":100.5,\"l\":100.38,\"c\":100.4},{\"o\":100.4,\"h\":100.46,\"l\":100.34,\"c\":100.4},{\"o\":101.3,\"h\":101.95,\"l\":101,\"c\":101.7},{\"o\":101.7,\"h\":101.8,\"l\":101.05,\"c\":101.15},{\"o\":101.15,\"h\":101.6,\"l\":101.1,\"c\":101.5},{\"o\":101.5,\"h\":102,\"l\":101.4,\"c\":101.8},{\"o\":101.8,\"h\":101.9,\"l\":101.35,\"c\":101.45},{\"o\":101.45,\"h\":101.85,\"l\":101.3,\"c\":101.75}],\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"zone\",\"from\":101,\"to\":102,\"role\":\"neutral\",\"label\":\"POST-RELEASE RANGE\"}]},\"entry\":102.05,\"units\":83},\"goal\":\"The spread is back to 0.10 and price breaks the range high: your buy fills at 102.05, 83 units. Drag the stop below the range and the target to 2R.\",\"start\":{\"stop\":101.5,\"target\":103,\"units\":83},\"locked\":[\"units\"],\"success\":{\"all\":[{\"field\":\"stop\",\"lt\":101},{\"field\":\"lossAfter\",\"lte\":100},{\"field\":\"rr\",\"gte\":2}]},\"feedback\":[{\"when\":{\"field\":\"stop\",\"gte\":101},\"text\":\"At {stop:2} the stop sits inside the post-release range, where a normal wobble can hit it. Put it below 101.00.\"},{\"when\":{\"field\":\"lossAfter\",\"gt\":100},\"text\":\"At {stop:2}, 83 units lose {lossAfter:2} with commissions: over 1R. Bring the stop up to just below the range.\"},{\"when\":{\"field\":\"rr\",\"lt\":2},\"text\":\"Stop right. The target sits at {rr:2}R; 2R is 2 × {stopDistance:2} above 102.05.\"}],\"correct\":\"Stop {stop:2}, just below the range: 83 units lose {lossAfter:2} there. Target {target:2} is {rr:2}R. Taken only once spreads normalised.\",\"hints\":[\"The range low is 101.00. The stop goes just below it.\",\"Stop 100.95 risks 1.10 a unit. 2R sits 2.20 above 102.05.\"],\"showMe\":{\"stop\":100.95,\"target\":104.25},\"concept\":\"second-move\"},\"check\":[\"q-2.10.8-1\",\"q-2.10.8-2\",\"q-2.10.8-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Act only once the spread is back under 2 × normal: 0.20 on LABX.\",\"Buy a break of the post-release range, stop at its other side, sized to 1R: 83 units.\",\"Pass on conflicting details, small surprises and pending press conferences: that's where the second move fails.\"],\"next\":\"Next, 2.10.9: one checklist for every order near news, and the shocks no calendar shows.\"}},\"2.10.9\":{\"unit\":\"2.10\",\"courseId\":\"2.10.9\",\"bridge\":\"From 2.10.1 to 2.10.8 you built the pieces: surprise, calendar, slippage, policies and the second move. Here you join them into one checklist, and face the shock no calendar shows, where, as with the weekend gap in 2.9.8, only size protects you.\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"Which of these protects you from an unscheduled shock?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"flat\",\"label\":\"Being flat before the CPI release\",\"feedback\":\"That handles CPI. An unscheduled shock comes at an hour nobody knew.\"},{\"id\":\"stop\",\"label\":\"A stop-loss\",\"feedback\":\"In a gap, a stop fills at the first price after the jump, which can be far beyond it.\"},{\"id\":\"size\",\"label\":\"A position small enough to survive a gap\",\"feedback\":\"Right.\"}],\"answer\":\"size\"},\"compare\":\"You chose {yours}. An unscheduled shock comes at an hour nobody knew, and a stop can fill far beyond its price. Only size and leverage cap the damage.\",\"concept\":\"news-risk-checklist\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"warning\",\"text\":\"Risk first: an [[unscheduled-event]], such as a war, an emergency rate move, a dropped currency peg or a flash crash, can't be dodged by being flat at a known hour. Stops fill into empty books. Your defence is size, leverage and holding nothing that can't survive a gap.\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Two real shocks\",\"text\":\"In 2015 the Swiss central bank dropped its cap on the franc without warning. EUR/CHF fell from 1.20 to well below 1.00 within minutes, stops filled far from their prices, and some brokers failed. In 2010 US stocks fell about 9% within minutes in a flash crash, then recovered much of it the same afternoon.\"},{\"kind\":\"reveal\",\"title\":\"Nothing on the calendar\",\"prompt\":\"LABX has been calm for days, as in the Arena's Unexpected event. You're long with a stop at 98.00, and nothing is on the calendar. Where does your stop fill?\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":99.9,\"h\":100.08,\"l\":99.84,\"c\":100.02},{\"o\":100.02,\"h\":100.14,\"l\":99.94,\"c\":100.1},{\"o\":100.1,\"h\":100.18,\"l\":100,\"c\":100.04},{\"o\":100.04,\"h\":100.1,\"l\":99.9,\"c\":99.96},{\"o\":99.96,\"h\":100.06,\"l\":99.88,\"c\":100},{\"o\":100,\"h\":100.12,\"l\":99.94,\"c\":100.08},{\"o\":100.08,\"h\":100.16,\"l\":100,\"c\":100.06},{\"o\":100.06,\"h\":100.1,\"l\":99.96,\"c\":99.98},{\"o\":99.98,\"h\":100.04,\"l\":99.86,\"c\":99.92},{\"o\":99.92,\"h\":100.02,\"l\":99.84,\"c\":99.98},{\"o\":99.98,\"h\":100.1,\"l\":99.92,\"c\":100.06},{\"o\":100.06,\"h\":100.14,\"l\":99.98,\"c\":100.02},{\"o\":100.02,\"h\":100.08,\"l\":99.92,\"c\":99.96},{\"o\":99.96,\"h\":100.06,\"l\":99.9,\"c\":100.04},{\"o\":100.04,\"h\":100.12,\"l\":99.98,\"c\":100},{\"o\":100,\"h\":100.06,\"l\":99.92,\"c\":99.98},{\"o\":85,\"h\":85.6,\"l\":84.4,\"c\":85.2},{\"o\":85.2,\"h\":86,\"l\":84.8,\"c\":85.7},{\"o\":85.7,\"h\":86.1,\"l\":85.3,\"c\":85.5}],\"visible\":16,\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":98,\"role\":\"stop\",\"label\":\"STOP 98.00\"}]},\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"at\",\"label\":\"At 98.00\",\"feedback\":\"Nothing traded at 98.00: the market reopened far below it.\"},{\"id\":\"near\",\"label\":\"A few cents below 98.00\",\"feedback\":\"That's ordinary slippage. Here price jumped 15% in one step.\"},{\"id\":\"first\",\"label\":\"At the first bid after the gap, far below\",\"feedback\":\"Right.\"}],\"answer\":\"first\",\"after\":\"The next price was 85.00, so the stop sold at a bid of 84.95: 13.05 below its level. The calendar, your timing and your stop all failed. Only the position's size decided the damage.\",\"concept\":\"news-risk-checklist\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Leverage decides the damage\",\"lines\":[\"Account 10,000. Effective leverage 10:1: an exposure of 100,000.\",\"A 15% gap against you: 0.15 × 100,000 = 15,000 lost.\",\"Balance after the gap: 10,000 − 15,000 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":-5000,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"vUSD\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":5000,\"text\":\"Check the sign: you lost more than the account held.\"},{\"value\":0,\"text\":\"That's with negative balance protection. The arithmetic first: 10,000 − 15,000.\"},{\"value\":8500,\"text\":\"That's the result at 1:1. At 10:1 the exposure, and the loss, are ten times bigger.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: −5,000.\"},\"after\":\"With [[negative-balance-protection]], which EU retail CFD clients have, the balance stops at 0; without it, you owe the 5,000. At 1:1 the same gap costs 1,500: 15% of the account. Painful, but survivable. More in 2.14.6.\",\"concept\":\"news-risk-checklist\"},{\"kind\":\"order\",\"title\":\"The checklist, in order\",\"prompt\":\"Put these checklist steps in the order you meet them during an event week.\",\"items\":[\"I know every red event for my instruments this week, in my time zone.\",\"My policy for each one is decided now, not at T−1 minute.\",\"If I hold, a gap of k × ATR stays within my maximum loss.\",\"After the release, I wait for normal spreads and prefer limit orders.\",\"I journal the slippage I paid, per event type.\"],\"explain\":\"Right: from the start of the week to the journal. The rest of the list applies throughout: a wider stop only with a smaller size, less size before weekends and holidays, and knowing your broker's news rules.\",\"concept\":\"news-risk-checklist\"},{\"kind\":\"fields\",\"title\":\"Your news rules\",\"prompt\":\"Set your own news rules. They're saved with this lesson; copy them into your trading plan.\",\"fields\":[{\"id\":\"window\",\"label\":\"Flat __ minutes before a red event\",\"kind\":\"choice\",\"options\":[5,15,30],\"unit\":\"min\",\"default\":15},{\"id\":\"k\",\"label\":\"When holding, survive a gap of __ × ATR\",\"kind\":\"stepper\",\"min\":2,\"max\":6,\"step\":1,\"default\":4},{\"id\":\"spread\",\"label\":\"After a release, act once the spread is at most __ × normal\",\"kind\":\"choice\",\"options\":[1.5,2,3],\"default\":2},{\"id\":\"journal\",\"label\":\"Your journal line for each event\",\"kind\":\"text\",\"default\":\"\",\"placeholder\":\"CPI: stop 98.00 filled at 97.40, slippage 0.30R\",\"required\":false}],\"valid\":{\"all\":[{\"field\":\"window\",\"gte\":15},{\"field\":\"k\",\"gte\":3}]},\"feedback\":[{\"when\":{\"field\":\"window\",\"lt\":15},\"text\":\"5 minutes is too tight: spreads start widening a minute or two before, and you need time to act calmly.\"},{\"when\":{\"field\":\"k\",\"lt\":3},\"text\":\"A 2 × ATR gap is a small one for a big release. Plan for 3 to 5 × ATR.\"}],\"correct\":\"Saved. These rules decide at T−15, so you don't have to.\",\"concept\":\"news-risk-checklist\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"key\",\"text\":\"When the calendar is silent, size is the only protection that works.\"}],\"task\":{\"widget\":\"bias-spotter\",\"data\":{\"options\":[{\"id\":\"policy\",\"label\":\"Policy decided in advance\"},{\"id\":\"gap\",\"label\":\"Gap within max loss\"},{\"id\":\"wider\",\"label\":\"Wider stop only with smaller size\"},{\"id\":\"spreads\",\"label\":\"Wait for normal spreads\"},{\"id\":\"weekend\",\"label\":\"Reduce before weekends and holidays\"},{\"id\":\"broker\",\"label\":\"Know your broker's news rules\"}],\"cards\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"text\":\"**Monday.** Wednesday's CPI is red. 'I'll see how I feel on the day,' you decide.\",\"answer\":\"policy\",\"correct\":\"Policy. Decided on the day means decided under pressure, at the worst time.\",\"rationales\":{\"gap\":\"The gap item fails when a k × ATR gap would cost more than 1R. Here the decision is pushed to the day itself.\",\"wider\":\"The wider-stop item fails when the stop widens but the size stays. Here the decision is pushed to the day itself.\",\"spreads\":\"The spreads item fails when an order goes in while spreads are still wide. Here the decision is pushed to the day itself.\",\"weekend\":\"The weekend item fails when full size rides into a long market close. Here the decision is pushed to the day itself.\",\"broker\":\"The broker item fails when you don't know its margin and stop rules for news. Here the decision is pushed to the day itself.\"}},{\"id\":\"b\",\"text\":\"**Holding 47 LABX** long, stop 98.00, into payrolls. ATR 0.80: a 4 × ATR gap would cost 155.03.\",\"answer\":\"gap\",\"correct\":\"Gap. 155.03 is over 1R. Cut to 30 units, or go flat.\",\"rationales\":{\"policy\":\"The policy item fails when the decision is left until the event is close. Here a 4 × ATR gap costs 155.03, over 1R.\",\"wider\":\"The wider-stop item fails when the stop widens but the size stays. Here a 4 × ATR gap costs 155.03, over 1R.\",\"spreads\":\"The spreads item fails when an order goes in while spreads are still wide. Here a 4 × ATR gap costs 155.03, over 1R.\",\"weekend\":\"The weekend item fails when full size rides into a long market close. Here a 4 × ATR gap costs 155.03, over 1R.\",\"broker\":\"The broker item fails when you don't know its margin and stop rules for news. Here a 4 × ATR gap costs 155.03, over 1R.\"}},{\"id\":\"c\",\"text\":\"**Before the rate decision** you move your stop from 98.00 to 96.00 to dodge the spike, and keep all 47 units.\",\"answer\":\"wider\",\"correct\":\"Wider stop, same size. A stop-out now costs 192.61 instead of 98.66.\",\"rationales\":{\"policy\":\"The policy item fails when the decision is left until the event is close. Here the stop doubles in distance while the size stays: a stop-out now costs 192.61.\",\"gap\":\"The gap item fails when a k × ATR gap would cost more than 1R. Here the stop doubles in distance while the size stays: a stop-out now costs 192.61.\",\"spreads\":\"The spreads item fails when an order goes in while spreads are still wide. Here the stop doubles in distance while the size stays: a stop-out now costs 192.61.\",\"weekend\":\"The weekend item fails when full size rides into a long market close. Here the stop doubles in distance while the size stays: a stop-out now costs 192.61.\",\"broker\":\"The broker item fails when you don't know its margin and stop rules for news. Here the stop doubles in distance while the size stays: a stop-out now costs 192.61.\"}},{\"id\":\"d\",\"text\":\"**Payrolls came out 40 seconds ago.** The spread is 0.80, and you send a market buy.\",\"answer\":\"spreads\",\"correct\":\"Spreads. At 0.80, eight times normal, you pay the news spread. Wait for 0.20 or less.\",\"rationales\":{\"policy\":\"The policy item fails when the decision is left until the event is close. Here the spread is still eight times normal.\",\"gap\":\"The gap item fails when a k × ATR gap would cost more than 1R. Here the spread is still eight times normal.\",\"wider\":\"The wider-stop item fails when the stop widens but the size stays. Here the spread is still eight times normal.\",\"weekend\":\"The weekend item fails when full size rides into a long market close. Here the spread is still eight times normal.\",\"broker\":\"The broker item fails when you don't know its margin and stop rules for news. Here the spread is still eight times normal.\"}},{\"id\":\"e\",\"text\":\"**Friday evening** before a three-day holiday. You hold your full swing size with a 2.00 stop.\",\"answer\":\"weekend\",\"correct\":\"Weekend. Three closed days give news three days to build a gap.\",\"rationales\":{\"policy\":\"The policy item fails when the decision is left until the event is close. Here full size rides into a long market close.\",\"gap\":\"The gap item fails when a k × ATR gap would cost more than 1R. Here full size rides into a long market close.\",\"wider\":\"The wider-stop item fails when the stop widens but the size stays. Here full size rides into a long market close.\",\"spreads\":\"The spreads item fails when an order goes in while spreads are still wide. Here full size rides into a long market close.\",\"broker\":\"The broker item fails when you don't know its margin and stop rules for news. Here full size rides into a long market close.\"}},{\"id\":\"f\",\"text\":\"**Your broker** raises margin before big releases. You've never checked by how much, or whether its stops are guaranteed.\",\"answer\":\"broker\",\"correct\":\"Broker rules. Check margin changes, order restrictions, guaranteed stops and negative balance protection before you need them.\",\"rationales\":{\"policy\":\"The policy item fails when the decision is left until the event is close. Here you don't know your broker's news rules.\",\"gap\":\"The gap item fails when a k × ATR gap would cost more than 1R. Here you don't know your broker's news rules.\",\"wider\":\"The wider-stop item fails when the stop widens but the size stays. Here you don't know your broker's news rules.\",\"spreads\":\"The spreads item fails when an order goes in while spreads are still wide. Here you don't know your broker's news rules.\",\"weekend\":\"The weekend item fails when full size rides into a long market close. Here you don't know your broker's news rules.\"}}]},\"goal\":\"Six order tickets near red events. Each breaks one item of the news checklist. Name the item it fails.\",\"success\":{\"field\":\"solved\",\"eq\":6},\"correct\":\"All six caught. Run the same list before every order near a red event: it takes a minute.\",\"hints\":[\"Ask what's wrong: the timing of the decision, the size, the stop, the spread or what you know about your broker.\",\"A gap costing over 1R fails the gap item; a wider stop with the same size fails the stop item.\"],\"showMe\":{\"solved\":6},\"concept\":\"news-risk-checklist\"},\"check\":[\"q-2.10.9-1\",\"q-2.10.9-2\",\"q-2.10.9-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Before any order near a red event, run the checklist: events, policy, gap size, spreads, journal.\",\"No timing dodges an unscheduled shock. Only small size and low leverage cap the damage.\",\"Journal the slippage you paid per event type: it's the real price of trading news.\"],\"next\":\"Next: the 2.10 checkpoint. Then unit 2.11, Portfolio risk and performance, because one event can hit all your positions at once.\"}}}");
